1. old measurements against the model (constant drag coefficient 0.47, sea-level air)
   Robins's musket ball: lead, 3/4 inch (19.1 mm), 1,700 feet a second (518 m/s); terminal velocity 70 m/s, ratio 7.40
      model at 45 degrees: 1164 m; vacuum 27369 m (17.0 miles). Robins: 'about 17 miles' in a vacuum, in fact 'short of half a mile' (805 m)
      drag at the muzzle in the model: 55 times the ball's weight. Robins measured about 120 times
   Robins's 24-pound shot: iron, 10.89 kg, 1,650 feet a second (503 m/s); terminal velocity 157 m/s, ratio 3.21
      model at 45 degrees: 4029 m; vacuum 25783 m (16.0 miles); model over vacuum 0.156. Robins: 'about 16 miles' in a vacuum, in fact 'less than three miles' (4,828 m), 'not ... the fifth part'; he cites a measured 2,250 French fathoms (4385 m)
   Helie's cannonball as given by Cohen and others: 15.1 kg at 485 m/s; in the model terminal velocity 166 m/s, ratio 2.93 (they give 2.05)
      model: greatest range 4485 m at 33.0 degrees; at 37.5 degrees 4455 m. Measured, as they give it: 5,690 m at 37.5 degrees
   Robins's musket ball with my sketched rise of drag near the speed of sound (0.47 below Mach 0.6, 0.95 above Mach 1.1): 1025 m at 45 degrees; reported 'short of half a mile' (805 m), by the writers he cites
   Robins's 24-pound shot with my sketched rise of drag near the speed of sound (0.47 below Mach 0.6, 0.95 above Mach 1.1): 3366 m at 45 degrees; reported 4,385 m
   Helie's cannonball with the paper's own ratio of 2.05 (terminal velocity 237 m/s, a drag coefficient of 0.23 for 15.1 kg of iron): greatest range 7071 m at 36.0 degrees; measured 5,690 m at 37.5
   the same ball with a drag coefficient of 0.30 (ratio 2.34): 5996 m at 37.5 degrees; greatest range 6011 m at 35.0 degrees (0.3% more than at 37.5)
   the same ball with a drag coefficient of 0.33 (ratio 2.46): 5641 m at 37.5 degrees; greatest range 5659 m at 34.5 degrees (0.3% more than at 37.5)
   the same ball with a drag coefficient of 0.36 (ratio 2.56): 5330 m at 37.5 degrees; greatest range 5351 m at 34.5 degrees (0.4% more than at 37.5)
   an iron ball of 8 cm, the diameter the paper prints, would weigh 2.1 kg, not 15.1
   the musket ball's place between the two pictures at other elevations (area under the path over range squared; parabola tan(a)/6, straight out and straight down tan(a)/2):
      15 degrees: 0.43;  30 degrees: 0.48;  45 degrees: 0.50;  60 degrees: 0.50;  75 degrees: 0.47
2. Cohen and others' closed forms, with L = vt^2/g and X = (v0/vt)^2:  range = (L/2) cos(a) ln(1 + 4 X sin(a));  best elevation = arctan sqrt(X / ((1 + X) ln(1 + X)))
